The second pharyngeal arch originates from the second pharyngeal arch, which develops during embryonic development. It differentiates into several important derivatives, including:  
1. **Costlohyoid muscle**: Derived from the lateral and medial parts of the second arch, it replaces the zygomatic process and__() the hyoid bone.  
2. **Facial vessels and nerves**: The second arch contributes to the superior pharyngeal vein and the deep cervical vein.  
3. **Parts of the hyoid bone**: The body and the body plate are derived from the second arch.  

Thus, the second pharyngeal arch plays a role in the formation of the hyoid bone and the surrounding musculature.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Embryological origin: Second pharyngeal arch} \\
&\text{Derivatives:} \\
&\quad \text{1. Costlohyoid muscle} \\
&\quad \text{2. Parts of the hyoid bone (body and body plate)} \\
&\quad \text{3. Facial vessels (superior pharyngeal vein and deep cervical vein)} \\
&\quad \text{4. Nerves (posterior belly of the vagus nerve)}
\end{aligned}
}

